  "body": "Separately authored audit of #1560's Theorem 1 (structural zero exclusion) and Corollary 2 (gauge form), and of how #1616 and #1650 use them. **Corroborated; no gap found.** No new \u03ba, bound or rigidity claim.\n\n#1560 (@an664, with GPT-6 Pro) states no independent human review. #1616 and #1650 apply its criterion at hundreds of pivot frames for the P107, P106 and P105 rigidity results, and cite the theorem without re-deriving it. This package checks that step.\n\n**What was checked**\n\n1. **Written audit of proof.md \u00a72\u2013\u00a74 and \u00a76**, step by step, over an arbitrary characteristic-zero field (README table). No gap. One expository omission is filled: \u00a76 says the pivot entries \"have the required form\" without the calculation. The README gives it, and Corollary 2 holds as stated.\n2. **Implicit finite facts at every pivot.** The proof uses E8 facts relative to F that it never states. #1560's checker covers only its own pivot. `verify.py` was written from the prose alone and checks these facts exactly at **all 2025 frames**, in about 50 s, standard library only:\n   - the expansion `2v_i = \u03a3 G_ik v_k`;\n   - `|T_i| = 4` and `G \u2208 {0, \u00b11}` off the pivot;\n   - the 14 blocks form an S(3,4,8) with 8 lines each;\n   - 4+4 orthogonal classes by sign product, each completing to a frame;\n   - Hadamard class rows;\n   - every exceptional 4+1 configuration (3,628,800) gives `w_j = \u00b11/2`;\n   - the class-sum dichotomy;\n   - `\u03a3_k \u03b5_ik \u03b5_jk = 2G_ij`.\n\n   Three negative controls are included.\n3. **Binding.** Both `criterion` functions were read against \u00a73. They test exactly (D), (G), (W) and nonempty blocks with the full live-frame family. For (D) they use a modular rank lower bound plus the seven kernel vectors, whose membership is identity (6), checked for every frame. `binding_crosscheck.py` compares #1650's `criterion` with a second implementation built on different exact methods: classes by orthogonality, Q-rank of `M\u1d40M` by Bareiss elimination, and BFS. All 4354 sampled cases agree (5\u201310-line deletions, including every live frame of five deletion sets), and so do 1503 thin-family cases (11\u201340-line deletions). The fixtures include session 13's full-family (G)-without-(D) pivot. The comparison is thin for (G) and (D), because #1650 stops at its first failing test.\n\n**Not covered:** #1212 \u00a78 (floor from gauge rigidity) and \u00a710 (stabiliser transport), which #1616/#1650's floors also need. Not covered either: whether (G) implies (D) (session 13's claim in #1502), or a proof-assistant formalization. Part 3 is a same-account check, since #1616/#1650 came from earlier Claude sessions on this account. Parts 1\u20132 do not use that code.\n\n**Task card:** `formal-interface-lemma` (chooser nonce `a2a3ecb2c0544e92b8d811b546d670c3`), scope refined to this theorem-to-certificate binding (TASK.json). Base main `a18b4b01`. Claim: https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/discussions/1502#discussioncomment-18916028\n\n**Checks run**\n- `python3 -I -B research/zero-exclusion-proof-audit/verify.py` on 3.10, 3.12 and 3.14 (also with `-O`).\n- `python3 scripts/research_package.py --package research/zero-exclusion-proof-audit --check-only`, plus the registered replay.\n- The cross-check as stated in the README.\n- A Codex read-only adversarial review found no mathematical gap. It raised three low-severity points (one control was really an illustration, the Python version was unstated, one overbroad phrase), all fixed in `c29103af`.\n\n**Credit:** Andrey Mas (@an664) with GPT-6 Pro for the theorem, proof and fixture. PR1560 also credits Brett Halligan / @burntout76 / Ninefold (#1212), @DreamingOfClouds (#717) and @themilkman216 (#968). Prepared by Josie Elliston with Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic) via Claude Code (substantial AI assistance). Apache-2.0.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
